<h3 id="cleanmymac">CleanMyMac</h3>
<p><em>Cost: $9.95/month ($3.33 if paid yearly), $119.95 one-time</em><br>
<em>Replacement: Mole (free)</em></p>
<p>CleanMyMac is a beautifully designed disk cleaning app. I don’t understand what it does or if it’s necessary, and I don’t know if it’s necessary to replace all its functions. So yeah, I’m completely ignorant and you should probably disregard everything I say about the technical side of keeping a Mac running well!</p>
<p>Anyway, I’ve replaced it with Mole, an open source Terminal app. I confess I’ve only done dry runs with it so far. Wondering if the people who call disk cleaning apps<span class="push-double"></span> <span class="pull-double">“</span>placebos” are right.</p>
<h3 id="cleanshot">Cleanshot</h3>
<p><em>Cost: $35.00</em><br>
<em>Replacement: Shottr (free to try, $12 to buy)</em></p>
<p>I loved Cleanshot, but Shottr is doing exactly what I need it to. No regrets with moving to a free tool for nice screenshots with a transparent background.</p>
<h3 id="due">Due</h3>
<p><em>Cost: $14.99 one-time</em><br>
<em>Replacement: Due on iOS, Reminders (free)</em></p>
<p>I bought the iOS Due app years ago, so I don’t need it on my Mac. Generally speaking I’ve found Reminders does very well at nagging if you set a reminder to repeat every hour. Or every 5 minutes, if it’s really important.</p>
<h3 id="hookmark">Hookmark</h3>
<p><em>Cost: Free / Standard $29.99 / Pro $69.99</em><br>
<em>Replacement: Free</em></p>
<p>Hookmark, a utility that lets you link anything with anything, is the missing link turning Apple devices into what they always should have been. I’ve written about it before.</p>
<p>My most common use case for Hookmark is for pasting links to graphics and files directly onto plain text project pages. It’s both cool and convenient to be able to create a Markdown table of resources for a project and fill the fields with deep links to PDFs, Affinity files, and folder locations.</p>
<p>There is no alternative to Hookmark that I’m aware of. I’ll be using the free version for now, upgrading to either Standard or Pro when the need arises. There’s an iOS version, too, that I have yet to explore.</p>
<h3 id="image2icon">Image2icon</h3>
<p><em>Cost: Free / $9.99</em><br>
<em>Replacement: Flairy (free)</em></p>
<p>I used Image2icon to make the folders of files I was selling look more interesting, and branded. Flairy claims to do the same thing. I’m bookmarking this one, and will try it out next time I release a new product. If the free version of Image2icon works well enough I’ll likely stick with it.</p>
<h4 id="section"></h4>
<h3 id="paste">Paste</h3>
<p><em>Cost: $29.99/year, $89.99/lifetime</em><br>
<em>Replacement: Raycast (free)</em></p>
<p>Paste is a visual clipboard manager. I’d never taken advantage of all its bells and whistles but I did use the basic functions many times a day.</p>
<p>Raycast’s clipboard history has been a good, though greatly simplified, replacement. I’ve mollified the ol’ muscle memory by setting the same hotkey I used with Paste. The free version of Raycast only lets you save 3 months worth though, and there’s none of Paste’s persistent collections.</p>
<p>When I’m ready for something more than Raycast (free version) provides I’ll probably go with PastePal ($14.99 for Pro), simply because it is so much cheaper than Paste to buy outright.</p>
<h3 id="popclip">PopClip</h3>
<p><em>Cost: $16.99</em><br>
<em>Replacement: PopClip $16.99</em></p>
<p>PopClip performs actions on the text you select. For me this was an instant one-time purchase of a utility that feels like an extension of my mind. I use it so often I forget it’s an add-on.</p>
<p>Most used functions:</p>
<ul>
<li>changing case<br>
</li>
<li>pop-up translation (mine is set to French)<br>
</li>
<li>thesaurus<br>
</li>
<li>character count<br>
</li>
<li>open link</li>
</ul>
<p>There are nearly 250 extensions to choose from, neatly divided into categories like Text Editing/Case/Transformation, Language and Translation, Utilities, Notes and Knowledge Management, and more.</p>
<h3 id="textsniper">TextSniper</h3>
<p><em>Cost: $9.99</em><br>
<em>Replacement: Shottr (see Cleanshot, above)</em></p>
<p>There are a lot of <span class="small-caps">OCR</span> options on Apple devices, including those built-in to the system. I tried out TextShot, but in the name of simplicity have settled into using Shottr. Keyboard shortcut + draw a marquee around the text = text on the clipboard.</p>
<p>TextSniper can do other things, I believe, but my brain isn’t ready to dive into those.</p>
<h3 id="typeface">Typeface</h3>
<p><em>Cost: $49.99</em><br>
<em>Replacement: FontBase (free)</em></p>
<p>Typeface is a capable font manager that is pleasant to use. The free version of FontBase is a very good replacement, for now.</p>
<p>The grid view that I appreciated in the Setapp version of Typeface is a paid feature of FontBase. I miss it, but not enough to pay for it! So far FontBase is doing what I need it to. Typeface has some nice additional design features, like font pairing and body text previews. I appreciated their existence but rarely used them.</p>
<p>If I were to buy the full version of either of these apps, there’s no question that my money would go to Typeface.</p>
<h3 id="squash">Squash</h3>
<p><em>Cost: $70/year</em><br>
<em>Replacement: Squoosh (free)</em></p>
<p>Squash does a good job but has a hefty price tag for a compression app. I’m replacing it with the open source Squoosh. ImageOptim is on my radar as well, because you can point it at a folder (like the image folder for my Blot.im hosted blog) and have it automatically optimise image sizes.</p>
<h2 id="adding-up-the-cost">Adding up the cost</h2>
<p>Total replacement cost: less than $30. I’ll give it a few months and report back how it’s going, and if I’m noticing any friction.</p>
<p>I’ll be especially interested to learn how my app-switching appetite handles this. In the past I’ve found Setapp quieted my app-shopping compulsion; hopefully that won’t reignite now that I no longer have a nice fat sack of apps to choose from.</p>
<h3 id="will-i-go-back-to-setapp">Will I go back to Setapp?</h3>
<p>Yes, I will probably return to Setapp. One day. When the brain has cleared and my explorer instinct reignites. Right now I’m putting the money I would have spent on that subscription into other areas of life, including using some of it to buy apps outright.</p>
<p>I recommend buying apps directly from the developers wherever possible rather than the App Store (which takes a 30% commission).</p>
<p>If Setapp sounds like a good fit for where you are at and you’d like to try it for a full 30 days rather than the standard 7, you can see if my <a href="https://setapp.sjv.io/c/4183813/344537/5114" target="_blank">referral link</a> (use the code <span class="small-caps">ELLANE</span>) is still working. I wouldn’t say no to a commission that comes at no extra cost to you. Otherwise seek out someone else’s referral and join via that.</p>
<p>Final word: Setapp is amazingly good value for the money. If you can afford it (and you’re not going to fall apart if you ever had to leave), I wholeheartedly recommend it.</p>
